<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!-- Exists so the Advance Stage button's visibility rule can be a PURE CHAIN OF ANDs.

         The rule this replaces was `1 AND 2 AND 3 AND (4 OR 5)`. A flexipage booleanFilter has no
         negation operator, so "hide at Prepare/Review unless approved" could only be written as its
         De Morgan equivalent — an OR inside parentheses. That deployed and survived a retrieve, but
         the button was still visible at Prepare/Review on an unapproved LOI, and whether the
         RENDERER honours those parentheses is not something this repo has ever confirmed. Moving the
         disjunction into a formula removes the question instead of re-litigating it: the visibility
         rule becomes two EQUAL comparisons, which are already proven to work on this very page
         (Submit for Approval's pure-AND rule behaves correctly).

         It also gives the logic ONE home. The same expression is what a validation rule on Stage__c
         would need in order to ENFORCE this rather than merely hide a button, which is the
         outstanding half of this work — a hidden headless quick action is a UX affordance and is
         bypassed by calling the Apex directly.

         ⚠ TEXT() compares, never ISPICKVAL: 'Prepare/Review' carries a literal slash. Same reason
         RecordStageAdvanceService's header gives for the future validation rules.

         ================================================================================
         D22 (2026-08-09) — THIS FIELD IS NO LONGER ACQUISITION-ONLY, AND THE `Executed`
         CLAUSE IS WHY.
         ================================================================================
         3B gave LOI__c a Disposition_LOI record type whose Stage__c value set ends at
         `Executed`, not `Completed`. The original formula excluded only `Completed`, so it
         returned TRUE at `Executed` — the DISPOSITION TERMINAL STAGE — and the Advance
         Stage button rendered on a finished disposition LOI and then refused the click.

         This is the SAME DEFECT as D20/C1 one object later: there, `NE 'Signed'` failed to
         exclude `Declined`; here, `<> "Completed"` fails to exclude `Executed`. Both come
         from a terminal-state test written against one record type's value set and then
         inherited by a second record type with a different one. When a third value set is
         ever added to this object, add its terminal value here in the same change.

         ZERO BEHAVIOUR CHANGE ON ACQUISITION. `Executed` is not a member of the
         Acquisition_LOI record type's Stage__c value set, so on an acquisition LOI the new
         clause is TRUE for every reachable stage and the field's value is unchanged for
         every acquisition row. Verified against the record-type value sets, not assumed.

         ⚠ DO NOT ADD A `RecordType.DeveloperName` TEST TO THIS FORMULA. It is not needed —
         the value sets are disjoint at the terminal stage, so naming both terminals is
         sufficient — and it would make a LIVE, WORKING acquisition button newly dependent
         on a merge field it does not use today. That is precisely the trade
         NDA_Record_Page rejected when it declined to reach for the same discriminator on
         Is_Decline_Allowed__c: adding a new dependency to a working path in order to fix an
         unrelated path is a regression risk taken for no gain.

         ⚠ The `Sent` clause remains ACQUISITION-SHAPED and is deliberately left alone.
         `Sent` is not on the disposition value set either, so it is inert there for the
         same reason `Executed` is inert on acquisition.

         ================================================================================
         🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-14 (Phase 3, observation 5, requirements C1). THE "DO NOT ADD A
         RecordType.DeveloperName TEST" INSTRUCTION DIRECTLY ABOVE IS WITHDRAWN, AND THE
         FORMULA NOW CARRIES ONE. THIS IS THE THIRD OCCURRENCE OF ONE DEFECT.
         ================================================================================

         RETRACTED, verbatim: "DO NOT ADD A `RecordType.DeveloperName` TEST TO THIS FORMULA.
         It is not needed — the value sets are disjoint at the terminal stage."

         ITS PREMISE DIED, IT WAS NOT OVERRULED. The instruction rested on the LOI value sets
         being disjoint. Observation 5 makes the acquisition sequence
         Draft -> Under Review -> Submitted -> Negotiation -> Signed, and 'Under Review' has
         been step 2 of the DISPOSITION sequence since 3B. It is now SHARED. Note the retracted
         sentence's own qualifier — "disjoint AT THE TERMINAL STAGE" — is STILL TRUE ('Signed'
         vs 'Executed'), which is exactly why the terminal clauses below needed no record-type
         guard and the approval clause did. The overlap is in the MIDDLE of the sequence.

         🔴 THREE BREAKS, AND ONLY THE SECOND ONE NEEDED THE RECORD TYPE.
         1. THE ACQUISITION TERMINAL MOVED 'Completed' -> 'Signed'. Without a `<> "Signed"`
            clause the formula returns TRUE at the new terminal, so Advance Stage renders on a
            FINISHED acquisition LOI and then refuses the click. That is the SAME DEFECT as
            D20/C1 (`NE 'Signed'` not excluding `Declined`) and D22 (`<> "Completed"` not
            excluding `Executed`), both recorded above in this same comment — a terminal-state
            test written against one value set and inherited by another. THIS IS THE THIRD
            OCCURRENCE. When a fourth value set or a fourth terminal appears, add it HERE, in
            the same change.
         2. 'Under Review' BECAME SHARED, AND THIS ONE INVERTS RATHER THAN LEAKS. The approval
            gate `OR(TEXT(Stage__c) <> "Prepare/Review", TEXT(LOI_Status__c) = "Approved")` was
            inert on disposition only because 'Prepare/Review' is not on that value set.
            Repointing it to 'Under Review' with no record-type test makes it LIVE there: a
            disposition LOI at 'Under Review' — step 2 of 5, the ordinary working state — would
            have Advance Stage hidden unless LOI_Status__c reads 'Approved', which nothing on
            the sell-side path ever writes. The sell-side path would dead-end at step 2 with no
            error and nothing logged. THIS is the clause that needed the discriminator, and it
            is the ONLY one that got it.
         3. 'Sent' -> 'Submitted' MOVED THE BRANCH POINT. The exclusion exists because the
            acquisition path BRANCHES there (two named buttons, Counter and Completed, rather
            than one generic Advance) — see RecordStageAdvanceService's header. The branch moves
            with the value: it is now at 'Submitted', whose two exits are 'Negotiation' and
            'Signed'. Both 'Sent' and 'Submitted' are acquisition-only, so this clause stays
            inert on disposition exactly as before and needs no guard.

         WHY THE GUARD IS SCOPED TO ONE DISJUNCTION AND NOT PUT AT THE TOP LEVEL. This field
         backs BOTH record types' Advance Stage entries on LOI_Record_Page (that is what D22
         established). A top-level `RecordType.DeveloperName = "Acquisition_LOI"` would return
         FALSE on every disposition LOI and delete the disposition Advance Stage button
         outright — reintroducing the exact 3B gap D22 closed. The guard belongs inside the one
         clause that is genuinely acquisition-semantic.

         WHY `RecordType.DeveloperName` AND NOT THE LOOKUP. It is the authoritative
         discriminator and it has in-repo precedent that has passed this org's check-only
         validation: NDA__c.Is_Decline_Allowed__c uses the same merge field, as does
         All_NDAs_Signed_Before_Progression. 🔴 IF THE DEPLOY REJECTS THE MERGE FIELD, the
         substitute is `NOT(ISBLANK(Disposition__c))` — i.e. the first disjunct becomes
         `NOT(ISBLANK(Disposition__c))` — and nothing else in this file changes. It is a NAME
         comparison, not an Id, so it survives an org rebuild but breaks if the Acquisition_LOI
         record type is ever renamed.
         ⚠ A MASTER-RECORD-TYPE ROW READS BLANK on this merge field, so the guard evaluates
         TRUE and the clause goes inert — the button shows. That is FAIL-OPEN and it is
         deliberate: RecordStageAdvanceService's defaultTypeKey routes a Master LOI to the
         ACQUISITION sequence, so showing the button is the correct answer for the population
         3B's post-deploy migration is supposed to have emptied anyway, and the server gate
         still refuses anything illegitimate.

         ⚠ THE OBJECTION IN THE RETRACTED PARAGRAPH IS ANSWERED, NOT IGNORED. It warned that a
         record-type test makes a live acquisition button "newly dependent on a merge field it
         does not use today". True, and the cost is bounded in a way the FLS objection behind
         NDA_Record_Page's version was not: `RecordType.DeveloperName` is not a custom field
         that ships with no permissions, and this file's FLS is unchanged by this edit. The
         alternative — leaving the clause unguarded — is a measured sell-side outage (break 2),
         not a hypothetical one.

         ⚠ THREE CLAUSES ARE TRANSITIONAL AND ARE REMOVED AT PHASE 3 D3, NOT NOW:
         `<> "Completed"`, `<> "Sent"` and the whole `Prepare/Review` disjunction. They name
         values that live acquisition LOIs still hold until the D1->D2 row migration completes,
         so naming BOTH the old and the new values makes this formula correct on BOTH sides of
         that migration and leaves NO window in either direction. This is the same
         list-both-then-drop shape Phase 2 used on Deal_Pipeline_by_Stage. Dropping them early
         re-opens break 1 for every un-migrated row.
         ⚠ THE COUNT IS THREE, AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN EDITORIAL. This paragraph read
         "FOUR" until 2026-08-14 while enumerating three, and apply-d3.js makes exactly three
         cuts to this formula (`Completed`, `Sent`, the `Prepare/Review` disjunction). An
         operator cross-checking the script against this comment on the one file the script must
         NOT over-cut would have found a count mismatch and had to guess which was wrong. If a
         fourth clause ever becomes transitional, change this number and add the matching edit to
         apply-d3.js in the SAME change.

         ⚠ inlineHelpText IS AT 241 OF 255 AND WAS OVER-CAP ON THE FIRST PASS, AT 258. That one
         element would have failed the whole atomic P3-D1 payload, including the Stage__c and
         Negotiation_Status__c values that [MIG], P3-D2 and P3-D3 all depend on. Two things were
         cut: the leading "System field. ", which is why this text now opens with the substance,
         matching Stage__c.inlineHelpText on this same object; and "(Signed or Executed)" became
         "(Signed/Executed)". Do NOT restore either without re-measuring with a parser: the cap on
         inlineHelpText is 255, NOT the 1000 that applies to <description> directly above it, and
         those two caps are easy to conflate on adjacent lines of the same file.

         ⚠ AND NOTE HOW THIS PARAGRAPH WAS FIRST WRITTEN, because it is the trap one layer up: the
         sentence explaining the over-cap fix contained a literal double hyphen, which an XML
         comment CANNOT contain, so the prose about a deploy defect introduced a deploy defect.
         Use a single hyphen, a semicolon or a reword. Never write two hyphens in a row anywhere
         inside a comment body in this repo, including inside a quoted CLI flag.

         =====================================================================================
         AMENDED 2026-08-21 (disposition LOI stage narrowing, user request). THE FORMULA IS
         UNCHANGED, NOT ONE CHARACTER OF THE formula BELOW WAS EDITED. ONLY THIS COMMENT, THE
         description AND THE inlineHelpText CHANGED. VERIFY BY DIFF, NOT BY THIS SENTENCE.
         =====================================================================================

         WHY NO FORMULA CHANGE WAS NEEDED. Disposition_LOI's terminal moved from 'Executed' to
         'Signed' (objects/LOI__c/recordTypes/Disposition_LOI.recordType-meta.xml). Clause 1,
         TEXT(Stage__c) &lt;&gt; "Signed", was ALREADY record-type-agnostic - it excludes
         'Signed' regardless of which record type carries it - so it already covers the new
         disposition terminal correctly with no edit. Re-verified against the new sequence: at
         Received/Under Review/Negotiation the formula is TRUE (button shown); at Signed, clause 1
         alone makes it FALSE (button hidden, correctly terminal on both record types now).

         CLAUSE 2, TEXT(Stage__c) &lt;&gt; "Executed", IS RE-ARGUED HERE, NOT JUST RE-STATED.
         THE ORIGINAL REASON FOR KEEPING IT IS PARTLY FALSIFIED BY A MEASUREMENT MADE THE SAME DAY.
         The design pass that authorised this change originally argued the clause stays live
         "because the repo's standing claim that record-type picklist restriction is UI-only means
         a data load or direct Apex write can still park a row on it." THAT PREMISE IS NOW
         MEASURED FALSE FOR ANY ROW THAT HAS A RECORD TYPE ASSIGNED: the developer agent reproduced
         INVALID_OR_NULL_FOR_RESTRICTED_PICKLIST inserting a Disposition_LOI row with
         Stage__c = 'Signed' before the record type's picklistValues block included it - this field
         is restricted=true (see valueSet on Stage__c), and restriction on a RESTRICTED value set
         IS enforced by Apex DML on this org. A direct write or data load of Stage__c = 'Executed'
         against an Acquisition_LOI- or Disposition_LOI-typed row would now be REJECTED at DML,
         not silently accepted - clause 2 cannot fire for either population.

         CLAUSE 2 IS KEPT ANYWAY, FOR A NARROWER AND STILL-REAL REASON: A MASTER-TYPE ROW. This
         field's own header, above, already establishes that a Master-record-type LOI reads BLANK
         on the RecordType merge field and is NOT constrained by either record type's
         picklistValues allow-list - there is no "Master record type's inclusion list" for the
         restriction to check against, so the DML enforcement just measured does not apply to a
         Master-typed row. 'Executed' stays ACTIVE on the master Stage__c field (orphaned, not
         deactivated - see that field's own 2026-08-21 retraction), so a Master-typed LOI
         genuinely CAN still be saved at Stage__c = 'Executed' today, by a data load, a direct
         Apex write, or a TestDataFactory fixture built without a record type (this repo's own
         established scratch-org fallback shape, cited in RecordStageAdvanceService's and
         NDA_Signed_Status_Sync's headers). For that one population, clause 2 is the only thing
         hiding Advance Stage at a stage with no legitimate next step, and removing it would let
         the button render on a Master-typed row stuck at 'Executed' with no clause left to catch
         it.

         WHAT THIS RETRACTS. Not the CONCLUSION ("keep clause 2") - that still holds - but the
         REASON. It is no longer a live guard against "a data load or direct Apex write" on the
         two real record types; it is a narrow guard against one specific degenerate shape,
         Master-typed rows. The distinction matters for the next reader deciding whether to
         remove it: removing it changes nothing observable for any Acquisition_LOI or
         Disposition_LOI row today, but does remove real coverage for the Master-row edge case.
         Revisit only if that edge case is itself ever closed off (e.g. by deactivating 'Executed'
         on the field, which this pass deliberately did not do). -->

    <fullName>Is_Advance_Allowed__c</fullName>
    <label>Is Advance Allowed</label>
    <description>Backs the Advance Stage button&apos;s visibility on the LOI record page, on BOTH record types. True when the generic one-hop advance is legitimate: not at a terminal stage (Signed, shared by both record types from 2026-08-21), not at the acquisition branch point (Submitted), not at the acquisition approval hop (Under Review) unless the LOI is Approved, and not while an approval is pending. The Under Review clause is guarded by RecordType because that value is SHARED with Disposition_LOI from 2026-08-14 - unguarded it would hide the sell-side button at step 2 of 5. The Executed clause is retained though Executed is now on no record type - see the XML comment for why.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>True when the Advance Stage button should be available on this LOI, on either record type. It turns off at the terminal stage (Signed), at the branch point (Submitted), and at Under Review on an acquisition LOI until it is approved.</inlineHelpText>
    <type>Checkbox</type>
    <formula><![CDATA[AND(
  TEXT(Stage__c) <> "Signed",
  TEXT(Stage__c) <> "Executed",
  TEXT(Stage__c) <> "Submitted",
  OR(
    RecordType.DeveloperName <> "Acquisition_LOI",
    TEXT(Stage__c) <> "Under Review",
    TEXT(LOI_Status__c) = "Approved"
  ),
  TEXT(LOI_Status__c) <> "Pending Approval"
)]]></formula>
    <formulaTreatBlanksAs>BlankAsBlank</formulaTreatBlanksAs>
</CustomField>
